WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.400
-Welcome back to
"The Tonight Show."

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.701
We're hanging out with
Kelly Ripa right here!

00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:06.701
The book is out right now.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:06.701 --> 00:00:08.501
In honor of your book,

00:00:08.501 --> 00:00:10.801
"Live Wire:
Long-Winded Short Stories,"

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:13.667
I thought we could put our
talking skills to the test

00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:16.767
in a new game called
"The Long-Winded Challenge."

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:23.634
-♪ "Long-winded Challenge" ♪

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:27.133
-Now --
-This game is sure to be a hit.

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.767
-Yeah, I know it is.
Yeah, watch.

00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:30.167
Here's how it works.
You'll like this.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:33.400
Each of us take turns picking
a card with a topic on it.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.467
Whatever topic you get,
you have to talk about it

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:38.300
for as long as you can
without taking any breaks.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.767
If you pause or say "um,"
you will be buzzed.

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.634
-I say "um" constantly.

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:45.868
-Not in this game.
-Okay.

00:00:45.868 --> 00:00:47.367
-Whoever can talk
the longest wins.

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:48.634
Are you ready for this?
-Ready.

00:00:48.634 --> 00:00:51.367
-Alright. I'm going to go first.
-Okay, you go first.

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:53.701
-Let me see. My subject is...

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.567
Okay. Can we get a timer?

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:02.868
I love submarines.

00:01:02.868 --> 00:01:05.067
They're almost like boats,
but they're underwater.

00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:06.400
And there was that
one movie with

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.634
Alec Baldwin and Sean Connery.

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:09.801
And they were
fighting the Russians,

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:11.133
and it was crazy.

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.234
I loved it so much, but I kind
of wish it was in a boat,

00:01:13.234 --> 00:01:15.634
because that would be more like
a fun summer movie,

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:16.734
where they're on
a boat together,

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:18.067
Sean Connery and Alec Baldwin.

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.734
I would love to see that, uh,
but I didn't see that.

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:21.167
Oh, I said "uh."
[ Buzzer ]

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:23.501
[ Cymbal crash, sad tuba plays ]

00:01:23.501 --> 00:01:24.801
-So hard.

00:01:24.801 --> 00:01:28.100
-I could have thought about
a submarine sandwich.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.234
-So, that's what I thought.

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:31.000
I was like,
"Submarine is a sandwich."

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.400
-I know.
I should have done sandwich.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.934
I could have talked so much
about submarine sandwiches.

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:36.033
I know, I went with that thing.

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:38.033
-I'm going to say "um"
in three seconds

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:39.801
because it only takes that long.

00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:41.000
-Let's see what your word is.
What if it's "um"?

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.968
-My word is New Jersey.

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:46.267
-Alright.

00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:50.667
-I love New Jersey
because it is the Garden State.

00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:53.267
A lot of people
don't realize that New Jersey

00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:55.667
was founded by
our forefathers...

00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:59.067
-Are you British?
-...many years ago.

00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:00.200
If I do not --
[ Buzzer ]

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.834
-No, don't give her
the buzz on that.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:04.501
Hey, that's not fair.
Don't give her the buzz on that.

00:02:04.501 --> 00:02:07.234
I interrupted her.
That's not fair.

00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:09.934
She was telling a beautiful
British story about New Jersey.

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:12.467
Keep the timer going.

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:16.033
-Nobody said that I was
not to use a British accent

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:17.601
at any point in this story.

00:02:17.601 --> 00:02:19.901
There are two parts of
New Jersey --

00:02:19.901 --> 00:02:24.000
North Jersey and I like to call
the superior South Jersey.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.300
-Oh, my God.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.133
-Near Philadelphia,
we are Philadelphia-adjacent,

00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:31.334
and, therefore,
we own Rocky Balboa.

00:02:31.334 --> 00:02:33.234
-What are you talking about?

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:35.033
-New Jersey is famous for its

00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:39.501
truck stops, rest stops,
Wawas, 7-Elevens,

00:02:39.501 --> 00:02:40.601
and, of course --
[ Buzzer ]

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:44.100
-Alright. Oh, my God.
That's not bad.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.801
Rocky Balboa?
What are you talking about?

00:02:46.801 --> 00:02:47.934
-I don't know.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:50.701
I had to talk about New Jersey,

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:52.367
which eventually
leads to Philadelphia.

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:53.934
-You crushed me on that one.

00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:55.901
Okay, it is time for
the final round.

00:02:55.901 --> 00:02:58.000
For this, we're going to talk
about the same topic,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.601
but alternate every 5 seconds.
-Every 5 seconds.

00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:01.801
-So when we hear this ding...

00:03:01.801 --> 00:03:03.100
[ Ding ]
...we'll alternate.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.434
The other person has to continue
where the other person

00:03:04.434 --> 00:03:08.601
left off and keep going until --
Whoever messes up loses.

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:09.767
-Okay, yeah.
-Here we go, bud.

00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:11.033
-Ready.

00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:14.234
-We're going to have to talk
about -- ooh! -- apples.

00:03:14.234 --> 00:03:16.934
-Apples.
-Okay. I can do this.

00:03:16.934 --> 00:03:18.701
Remember, switching off
every 5 seconds.

00:03:18.701 --> 00:03:21.901
So, I'll start us off.
[ Laughs ]

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.834
I love apples so much.

00:03:23.834 --> 00:03:27.767
Green ones, red ones,
yellow ones.

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:28.968
[ Ding ]

00:03:28.968 --> 00:03:32.334
-Granny Smith apples
are the greatest apples

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:33.934
in all of the orchard.

00:03:33.934 --> 00:03:37.167
-Of course, the apple originated
by Johnny Appleseed.

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:38.434
[ Ding ]

00:03:38.434 --> 00:03:41.601
-Did you know that
you can shoot an apple off of

00:03:41.601 --> 00:03:43.300
someone's head
with nothing more --

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.534
-Do I know
or have I experienced it?

00:03:45.534 --> 00:03:47.334
Yes, of course, I have gotten
an apple shot off my head.

00:03:47.334 --> 00:03:48.767
[ Ding ]

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:52.234
-You can use grenade to blow up
a whole orchard of apples.

00:03:52.234 --> 00:03:53.267
[ Ding ]

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:55.133
-Now you're speaking
about applesauce.

00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:56.567
And that's how it was invented.

00:03:56.567 --> 00:03:59.234
-I like apple pie,
not just sauce.

00:03:59.234 --> 00:04:00.400
-I'm going to give you
the win on this.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.501
Come on. Kelly Ripa.

00:04:01.501 --> 00:04:02.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:04:02.934 --> 00:04:05.300
The British accent -- Why did
the British accent come out?

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.567
-It's the only way --
-That's the only we enjoy it.

00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:09.501
I love you. Kelly Ripa.

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:12.834
Her book, "Live Wire:
Long-Winded Short Stories,"

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:15.434
is available now
wherever books are sold.

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:17.067
We'll be right back
with Kid Cudi.

00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:18.133
Come on back.

